Nicole Sullivan, known online as "Stubbornella," is a front-end developer, performance expert and CSS architect.

## Performance at Yahoo!

At Yahoo! she worked to build a culture of performance and quality among front-end developers, helping teams make big websites load faster. She contributed to *Even Faster Web Sites*, the follow-up to Steve Souders's influential book on web performance.

## Object-Oriented CSS

Large sites often ended up with huge, tangled style sheets. Sullivan's answer was Object-Oriented CSS (OOCSS), which she presented in 2009. It treats repeating parts of a page, like a media block with an image and text, as reusable "objects" with their own classes. Its two main principles are:

- **Separate structure from skin:** Keep layout rules apart from colors and decoration.
- **Separate container from content:** Style an object the same way wherever it appears.

OOCSS influenced later CSS methodologies such as SMACSS.

## Consulting and community

After Yahoo!, she started a consultancy and helped companies such as Facebook shrink bloated style sheets. With Nicholas Zakas she created CSS Lint, a tool that flags problems in CSS. She also founded CSSConf, which held its first event in Florida in 2013.

